# Python Runtime Gating, Windows Unship, and 1.0.0 Supported Surface

Status: approved, not yet implemented
Created: 2026-08-15

## Decisions in force

- Gate the Python runtime at dispatch only
- Narrow `disabled_runtimes` list as the single lever
- Version 1.0.0
- No drift guard for the frozen Python tree
- Windows launchers unshipped but retained
- Parity suite skipped, not deleted
- The `python/` tree is frozen

A consequence of the dispatch-only choice, which supersedes the earlier
"hard error at load" phrasing: the persisted value `python` continues to
validate and load normally in every code path. The refusal happens only where a
Python process would be started. This means no file under `python/src/` is
modified by this plan, which is what keeps the tree genuinely frozen.

## 1. The gate lever

### 1.1 Modified: `shared/global-config-rules.json`

Add a top-level key `disabled_runtimes` with value `["python"]`, placed
immediately after `supported_runtimes`.

This is the single authoritative list. Re-enabling Python later is the act of
emptying this array.

`supported_runtimes` is not changed. `python` remains a valid, loadable
configuration value, which is what preserves recoverability.

### 1.2 Not modified

`schemas/global-config.schema.json` keeps `"enum": ["groovy", "python"]`.
Narrowing it would reject existing configuration files at validation time,
reintroducing the deadlock.

No file under `python/src/ai_worklog_framework/` is touched, including
`global_config.py`, `setup/resolver.py`, `setup/commands.py`, and `cli.py`.
Their internal `SUPPORTED_RUNTIMES` handling is left exactly as it stands.

## 2. Dispatch refusal

### 2.1 Modified: `bin/ai-worklog`

Add a helper `_read_disabled_runtimes` following the existing
`_read_config_runtime` structure: attempt the Python one-liner first, fall back
to a Groovy one-liner, both reading `disabled_runtimes` from
`shared/global-config-rules.json` and printing it space-separated. On total
failure it prints nothing and returns success, so an unreadable rules file
never blocks the Groovy path.

Insert the gate in the `case "$RUNTIME"` block at lines 92–103, evaluated only
when `$RUNTIME` is not `groovy`. This keeps the hot path free of any additional
subprocess.

When the resolved runtime appears in the disabled list, print to stderr, in
this order:

- a first line naming the runtime and stating it is disabled in this build
- a second line stating the source of the selection, distinguishing `--runtime`, `AI_WORKLOG_RUNTIME`, and the persisted global configuration
- a third line giving the literal recovery command `ai-worklog --runtime groovy config runtime groovy`, shown only when the selection came from persisted configuration

Exit with the user-error code 1.

The recovery command works because an explicit `--runtime` is parsed before any
configuration is read, and `groovy` is not in the disabled list.

### 2.2 Modified: `bin/ai-worklog-python`

Direct invocation currently bypasses the dispatcher entirely. Add a guard at
the top of the script, after `ROOT` is established, that reads the disabled
list using the same Groovy-then-Python fallback pattern and, when `python` is
listed, prints the same refusal message shape and exits 1 before any
interpreter is selected.

### 2.3 Modified: `bin/ai-worklog-groovy`

Line 27 currently reads `Groovy is required for the default runtime. Use
--runtime python as fallback.` Replace the second sentence so it no longer
advertises a disabled runtime; the message must state only that Groovy is
required and name the `AI_WORKLOG_GROOVY` and `AI_WORKLOG_JAVA_HOME`
overrides.

## 3. Refusing to create a new deadlock

Setting a disabled runtime is refused at the command level in the Groovy
implementation, while validation of an already-persisted value continues to
succeed. This prevents users from writing themselves into the broken state
going forward, without invalidating configurations that already exist.

### 3.1 Modified: `groovy/src/main/groovy/ai/worklog/framework/core/GlobalConfig.groovy`

- Add a constant `DISABLED_RUNTIMES` derived from `RULES.disabled_runtimes`, defaulting to an empty set, alongside `ALLOWED_RUNTIMES` at line 18
- Add `disabled_runtimes` with default `['python']` to the inline fallback map inside `loadRules` at line 635
- In `setRuntime` at line 429, after the existing `ALLOWED_RUNTIMES` check, raise `IllegalArgumentException` when the requested runtime is in `DISABLED_RUNTIMES`, with a message naming the runtime as disabled in this build
- `validate` at line 96 is not changed, so loading a persisted `python` value still succeeds

### 3.2 Modified: `groovy/src/main/groovy/ai/worklog/framework/setup/SetupResolver.groovy`

In `validateRuntime` at line 81, return false when the runtime is in
