## 9.4 Test harness

Verified 2026-08-15. `groovy/build.gradle` pins a Java 17 toolchain, but this
machine has only JDK 26 (`/opt/java/openjdk-26`) and Gradle is not installed,
with no wrapper in the repository. Gradle is not a usable entry point here.

The Groovy distribution at `/opt/groovy/current/lib` bundles `junit-4.13.2.jar`
and `hamcrest-core-1.3.jar`, so the suite runs directly. It must be run from
`groovy/`, because `CoreTest.setUp` resolves the repository root as
`new File('..')` — an assumption Gradle satisfied implicitly through its
working directory. Running from the repository root instead produces three
spurious `CoreTest` failures that resemble catalog, state, and redaction bugs
but are purely path resolution.

Baseline: 118 tests, 0 failures, JDK 26, Groovy 6.0.0-beta-1.

```bash
cd repos/ai-worklog-framework/groovy
AI_WORKLOG_FRAMEWORK_ROOT="$(cd .. && pwd)" groovy \
  -cp "src/main/groovy:src/test/groovy" \
  -e 'def names = ["CoreTest","DiagnosticsTest","GlobalConfigTest","JenkinsOperatorTest","ReconciliationTest","SetupTest","WorkspaceStateTest"]
      def r = org.junit.runner.JUnitCore.runClasses(names.collect { Class.forName("ai.worklog.framework." + it) } as Class[])
      println "run=${r.runCount} failures=${r.failureCount}"
      r.failures.each { println "FAIL: ${it.testHeader} :: ${it.message}" }'
```

This is a gap, not a solution: the release gate for 1.0.0 currently depends on
a hand-assembled classpath rather than a supported build entry point, and
`build.gradle` advertises a toolchain that cannot be satisfied. Section 7.2
must record this, and section 7.3 must carry it as a deferred item with its own
restore steps, namely adding a Gradle wrapper or a `scripts/run-tests.sh` and
reconciling the declared Java toolchain with the JDK actually in use.

